{
 "lead": "A rectifier alone gives pulsating DC. A capacitor across the load stores charge near each peak and feeds the load in the gaps; a series inductor slows every change of current. Only a few syllabi list this topic, but almost every mains-powered supply starts with one of these filters.",
 "prereq": "You need: the full-wave bridge and its ripple at 2f from <a href=\"#diode-rectifiers-half-wave-full-wave-and-bridge\">diode rectifiers</a>.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Shunt capacitor filter",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Connect a capacitor C across the load. While the rectified voltage climbs to Vm the diodes conduct and charge C to Vm. Once the rectified voltage falls below the capacitor voltage, every diode is reverse biased and C alone feeds the load. The output becomes a small sawtooth just below Vm."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vr = Idc/(2fC) (full wave);   Vr = Idc/(fC) (half wave)",
     "where": "Vr is the peak-to-peak ripple in V, Idc the load current in A, f the supply frequency in Hz, C in F; it assumes C discharges almost linearly for the whole gap."
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "Vdc ≈ Vm − Vr/2;   RF = Vr/(2√3 Vdc) = 1/(4√3 f C R) (full wave)",
     "where": "R is the load resistance in Ω. The sawtooth's RMS value is Vr/(2√3)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Ripple grows with load current, so a capacitor filter suits light loads. The diodes conduct only for a few tens of degrees, so the charge arrives in tall narrow pulses that distort the supply current (see <a href=\"#power-factor-correction-pfc-rectifiers\">PFC rectifiers</a>). The PIV is unchanged in full-wave circuits but rises to 2Vm in a half-wave circuit: the diode blocks the capacitor's +Vm plus the supply's −Vm."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Series inductor and L-C filters",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "An inductor L in series with the load presents a reactance 2πf_r L to the ripple, where f_r is the ripple frequency (2f for full wave), so doubling L halves the ripple current. It works best at heavy load, where the current never stops."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The L-C (choke-input) filter puts L in series and C across the load: L blocks the ripple current and C bypasses what is left. With a full-wave input the output stays at Vdc = 2Vm/π, not Vm, and"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "RF = √2 / (12 ω² L C)",
     "where": "ω = 2πf is the supply angular frequency in rad/s, L in H and C in F; valid when the choke current is continuous."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The choke current stays continuous only while L is above the <b>critical inductance</b> Lc = R/(3ω). For R = 100 Ω on 50 Hz, Lc = 0.106 H; with L = 0.2 H and C = 1000 µF, ω²LC = 19.74 and RF = 1.4142/(12 × 19.74) = 0.6 %. At light load Lc rises above L and the output climbs towards Vm; a bleeder resistor prevents this and discharges C after switch-off."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A bridge rectifier on 230 V, 50 Hz feeds a 2 A load through a 1000 µF capacitor. Estimate the ripple, Vdc, the ripple factor and the load resistance.",
   "steps": [
    "Vm = 230 × √2 = 325.27 V.",
    "Vr = Idc/(2fC) = 2 / (2 × 50 × 1000 × 10⁻⁶) = 20.0 V peak to peak.",
    "Vdc ≈ 325.27 − 10.0 = 315.27 V, so R = 315.27/2 = 157.6 Ω.",
    "RF = Vr/(2√3 Vdc) = 20.0/(3.464 × 315.27) = 0.018, against 0.482 with no filter.",
    "Check: RC = 0.158 s, sixteen ripple periods. A step-by-step simulation gives 17.9 V because C recharges about 19° before each peak, so the formula errs on the safe side."
   ],
   "answer": "Vr ≈ 20 V, Vdc ≈ 315.3 V, RF ≈ 0.018, R ≈ 157.6 Ω."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Using 2f in the half-wave ripple formula. Half wave uses f; full wave uses 2f.",
  "Saying the capacitor never changes the PIV. A half-wave circuit's PIV doubles to 2Vm.",
  "Giving Vdc = 2Vm/π for a capacitor filter. That belongs to the choke-input filter; with C the output sits near Vm.",
  "Treating a bigger capacitor as free improvement: it raises the peak diode current."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the full-wave capacitor-filter ripple formula and name its symbols.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Vr = Idc/(2fC): peak-to-peak ripple Vr, load current Idc, supply frequency f, capacitance C."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a capacitor filter suit light loads and a series inductor heavy loads?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Capacitor ripple grows with Idc; an inductor smooths best when its current is large and continuous."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A half-wave rectifier with C = 470 µF at 50 Hz delivers 0.5 A. Find the ripple.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Vr = Idc/(fC) = 0.5/(50 × 470 × 10⁻⁶) = 21.3 V peak to peak."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "Doubling C raises Vdc only a little but raises the peak diode current a lot. Explain.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "The charge per ripple cycle, Idc/(2f), is fixed. A larger C shortens the conduction angle, so the same charge flows in a taller pulse; Vdc cannot pass Vm and gains only Vr/4."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "An L-C filter's output rises towards Vm when the load is removed. Why, and what fixes it?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "At light load Lc = R/(3ω) exceeds L, the choke current breaks up and C charges to the peak. A bleeder resistor keeps L above Lc."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. A real DC-link capacitor is chosen by its ripple-current rating and lifetime, because the charging pulses heat it. Search for 'ripple current rating' and 'hold-up time' next.",
